# Jam Bug Analysis

When working with Jam bug reports, follow these guidelines to investigate issues effectively.

## Getting Started

- Always start with `getDetails` when given a Jam URL or ID — it provides the bug report overview, device info, and investigation guidance.
- Follow the **Investigation Guide** returned by `getDetails` — it recommends which tools to call next based on signals detected in the report.

## Jam Identifiers

- Jam URLs look like `https://jam.dev/c/<shortId>` or contain a UUID.
- You can pass either a full URL or a UUID to any tool's `jamId` parameter (or `id` for `fetch`).
- Use `search` if you have a URL or pasted text and need to resolve it to a Jam — it returns empty results when no UUID/URL can be extracted from the query. It only matches video Jams: a screenshot or replay Jam that exists still comes back empty, so use `searchJams` for those.
- `fetch` is an alias for `getDetails` (kept for tools that expect the standard MCP `fetch` shape).

## Investigation Tools

Use these tools to drill into specific aspects of a bug report:

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `getDetails` | Overview, metadata, device info, investigation guidance |
| `getNetworkRequests` | HTTP requests — filter by `statusCode`, `method`, `host`, `contentType` |
| `getConsoleLogs` | Browser console output — filter by `logLevel` (error, warn, info) |
| `getUserEvents` | Timeline of clicks, inputs, navigation, scrolls |
| `getScreenshots` | Visual screenshots (screenshot-type Jams only) |
| `getFrames` | Still frames from video Jams as images — `overview: true` for a labeled grid across the whole video, or sample by `at` timestamps / `fromMs`–`toMs` window (max 30 frames/call) |
| `analyzeVideo` | Extract user intents from video recordings |
| `getVideoTranscript` | Speech transcript from video Jams (if mic was enabled) |
| `getVideoChapters` | AI-generated chapters for a video Jam — titled segments with start/end timestamps |
| `getMetadata` | Custom metadata set via `jam.metadata()` SDK |

## Discovery & Management Tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `searchJams` | Search workspace Jams with `v: 1`, `text`, structured `where` filters, `window`, and `page` |
| `listJams` | Deprecated legacy search; use `searchJams` |
| `listFolders` | Browse team folders |
| `listMembers` | Find team members (useful for filtering by author) |
| `createFolder` | Create a folder to file Jams into |
| `updateFolder` | Rename a folder (does not move Jams) |
| `createComment` | Add a comment to a Jam (supports Markdown) |
| `editComment` | Rewrite a comment you authored |
| `addReaction` | React to a comment on a Jam |
| `removeReaction` | Take back a reaction you left |
| `updateJam` | Rename a Jam, rewrite its description, or move it to a different folder (title and description edits need an Admin or Creator seat) |

Use `searchJams` for discovery. Its `where` entries are ANDed `{ field, op, value }` leaves. For example, `{ v: 1, text: "checkout", window: { from: "-P7D" }, where: [{ field: "author", op: "eq", value: "me" }] }`. Continue with the returned `page.cursor` in the next request's `page.cursor` while `page.hasMore` is true. Return each Jam's `url` unchanged.

## Feedback

`submitFeedback` sends feedback to the Jam team. Show the user the exact text and get approval before calling it. Send at most once per session, with a body under 200 words. Never include secrets, customer data, source code, or stack traces. Do not report a mistake you corrected yourself.

## Recording Link Tools

These manage reusable recording links (URLs anyone can use to record and submit a Jam). They are management tools, not investigation tools — use them when asked to set up or administer bug-report collection, not when diagnosing a Jam.

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `createRecordingLink` | Create a reusable recording link (optional target folder, expiration) |
| `listRecordingLinks` | List the team's recording links (cursor pagination) |
| `getRecordingLink` | Get one link by `publicId`, including how many Jams it collected |
| `updateRecordingLink` | Rename a link, change its folder/expiration/metadata |
| `deleteRecordingLink` | Revoke a link (soft delete — recorded Jams are untouched) |
| `listRecordingLinkJams` | List the Jams recorded through a link |
| `listRecordingUrls` | List the team's connected domains (recording URLs) |
| `getRecordingUrlVerifyLink` | Get a browser link a human opens to verify a connected domain |

Important: a recording link only captures console and network logs when it starts from a **verified connected domain**. Call `listRecordingUrls` first and ask the user which domain to record from — a link created without a `recordingUrlId` captures no logs.

## Destructive Tools

These permanently remove data. Nothing on the team can restore it — the dashboard has no trash or
archive view. Confirm with the user before calling any of them, and never claim the data can be
recovered afterwards.

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `deleteJam` | Delete a Jam |
| `deleteComment` | Delete a comment you authored, along with its attachments |
| `deleteFolder` | Delete a folder and every Jam inside it |
| `deleteRecordingLink` | Revoke a recording link (soft delete — recorded Jams are untouched) |

## Best Practices

1. **Follow the server's guidance.** The `getDetails` response includes an Investigation Guide with prioritized next steps — follow it instead of running every tool blindly.
2. **Prefer the transcript for video Jams with mic.** When the reporter narrated, `getVideoTranscript` is higher priority than `analyzeVideo` — it's faster and captures intent directly.
3. **Orient visually with `getFrames`.** For video Jams, `getFrames` with `overview: true` gives a timestamp-labeled grid of the whole recording in one call — cheaper than `analyzeVideo` when you just need to see what was on screen. Follow up with `at` timestamps around the moment of failure.
4. **Filter aggressively.** Use `statusCode: ["4xx", "5xx"]` or `logLevel: ["error"]` to focus on failures instead of fetching everything. The `statusCode`, `contentType`, `host`, `method`, and `logLevel` filters take arrays. Values are ORed within a filter and ANDed across filters. Use `limit` to cap output. If the returned page has fewer entries than `total`, narrow the filters or pass `nextCursor` as `after`. Network bodies default to failed requests; request `bodies: "all"` to inspect successful responses. Bodies are truncated to 4KB and flagged per row.
5. **Correlate across data sources.** Match network errors with console errors and user events to build a timeline of what went wrong.
6. **Check the Jam type first.** `getScreenshots` only works for screenshot Jams; `analyzeVideo` short-circuits on non-video Jams; `getVideoTranscript` requires that the mic was enabled.
7. **Handle "not ready" responses.** Event-backed tools (network, console, user events, transcript, intents) can return a postprocessing-pending message on fresh Jams. Retry after a few seconds or fall back to data that's already available.
8. **Provide structured diagnoses.** Summarize findings with: what happened, probable root cause, supporting evidence, and recommended next steps. Post them back via `createComment` only when the user asks.

Video chapters and timestamped video comments can include a public `thumbnailUrl` for sharing with a person. Use `getFrames` to inspect the video yourself, preferably at a chapter midpoint. After fixing the bug, use `jam skills get jam-proof` for CLI guidance on recording or uploading proof.
